# Implement continuous contextual stroke planning

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical notes below identify existing domain primitives but do not prove
that the proposed finite synthesis contract is executable.

Select, connect, deform, space, and lift strokes from neighboring graphemes,
word position, line geometry, semantic role, and calibrated writing style.

Current executable evidence now freezes both sides of the transport-neutral
planner boundary. Profile-provided contextual candidates retain caller-owned
candidate identity, character intent, stroke-vocabulary payload, semantic
origin, profile choice, and entry/exit conditions. One complete planning input
also retains neighboring graphemes, word position, line geometry, semantic role,
and calibrated writing style while preserving caller candidate order.


A pure identity-collision check can now reject the first repeated candidate
identity and report its earliest prior owner without selecting, ranking, or
rewriting candidates. A complete planning input has an explicit validator that
applies the same invariant while leaving candidate order and context unchanged;
construction still has no implicit validation, selection, or fallback side
effect.

Successful admission now also returns constructor-sealed evidence borrowing the
exact input, caller-order candidate slice, and planning context. A 1,093-case
compact oracle exhausts every three-identity sequence through length six and
requires raw-candidate, complete-input, and sealed-input outcomes to agree on
the exact first duplicate and earliest prior owner.

The inspectable output plan keeps ordered samples with caller-owned position,
tangent, curvature, width or pressure proxy, velocity, and explicit contact
state; each declared stroke retains semantic origin, profile choice, and
contextual entry/exit conditions. Empty declared strokes reject before any
projection, while an empty plan remains valid for content with no handwriting.
A constructor-sealed validated plan can project the exact planner-order stroke
indices owned by one semantic origin repeatedly without rescanning structural
validity, providing an edit-local dependency region without mutating or
replanning the plan. An exhaustive 6,561-state eight-stroke oracle crosses
empty, origin-A, and origin-B strokes; it pins first-empty precedence across
6,305 invalid states and exact provenance projections across all 256 valid
states.

Candidate ranking/selection, invalidation orchestration, geometry, units, joins,
deformation, spacing, interpolation, rendering, PDF, and machine-motion behavior
remain open.

## Finite deterministic mixed-letter contextual line plan

**Inputs:** accepted source text `la ma`, one selected profile revision,
semantic text owner, measured baseline and glyph-coverage declarations,
contextual stroke candidates, writing role and style. **Outputs:** one ordered
and replayable stroke/contact plan preserving source provenance, or a typed
`MissingCandidate`, `UnsupportedJoin`, `Collision`, or `InvalidBounds` refusal.
No renderer, G-code or physical device motion follows directly from the plan.

**Finite candidate rule:** segment exactly four letters and one separating
space without Unicode normalization. For each letter, choose only an admitted
candidate whose declared left/right context and entry/exit conditions match
the adjacent graphemes. Order alternatives by caller-declared candidate rank
then stable candidate identity; duplicate identities refuse before ranking.
Joins require compatible continuous pen contact and tangent difference within
a proposed 15-degree threshold; otherwise require an explicit lift and
unconnected next stroke. Word spaces always force a lift and an admitted
measured gap. Never join through punctuation, a missing glyph or a page edge.

**Worked plan:** in body role, the accepted profile provides isolated `l`,
`a`, `m` and contextual pairs `l-a` and `m-a` with compatible connection
conditions. A 3.0 mm body height and 1.0 mm word gap yield the following
proposed order:

| Step | Semantic owner | Contact/geometry decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `l` in word 1 | Start contact; use joined-left entry |
| 2 | `a` in word 1 | Join `l` to `a`, tangent 8 degrees |
| 3 | Word space | Lift; advance 1.0 mm |
| 4 | `m` in word 2 | Set pen down on measured baseline |
| 5 | `a` in word 2 | Join `m` to `a`, tangent 11 degrees |
| 6 | End of line | Lift; preserve both word owners |

**Recomputation rule:** each stroke keeps the exact selected candidate,
semantic owner and measured physical coordinate system. A change only to the
second word invalidates its candidate/join plan and possibly later baselines;
unchanged first-word provenance remains stable until measured line layout
changes. Determinism means identical accepted source, profile revision,
geometry and candidate list lead to identical ordering and contact decisions.

**Refusal example:** if the selected `m` candidate exits at a 40-degree
incompatible tangent, join is refused and a declared lift path may be used
only if that profile permits separate letter shapes. No compatible candidate,
unknown glyph, duplicated candidate ID, missing baseline, or contour crossing
returns an exact failure; no hidden substitute stroke or unbounded search.

**Assumptions and limits:** typed contextual candidates, duplicate checks,
provenance-preserving stroke output and local owner projection already exist.
Selection/ranking, tangent metrics, actual deformation, curve interpolation,
spacing and path rendering do not. The above six-step plan and degree
thresholds are theoretical examples, not generated physical strokes.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for typed planning inputs/outputs and provenance;
  theoretical for selection, joins and geometry, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one ordered six-step two-word plan, finite candidate and
  joining policy, dependency scope and unsupported-case refusals.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** real contextual ranking, geometric
  joins, handwriting synthesis, rendered PDF or machine motion.
